window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"t": "Academic Help", "u": "academic-help/", "k": "", "s": "Advising, tutoring, and mentoring to keep you on track.", "x": "Advising, tutoring, and mentoring to keep you on track. Academic Advising Plan your degree with your assigned advisor. Find my advisor Tutoring Free drop-in peer tutoring in SEH 130. See tutoring Mentoring Connect with a peer or working engineer. Explore mentoring Other WSU academic support Academic Success and Career Center : peer-facilitated drop-in tutoring Math Learning Center : tutor-assisted study halls Writing Center : peer help with writing assignments Chemistry Learning Center : homework help and test prep eTutoring : online help for undergraduates"}, {"t": "Academic Advising", "u": "academic-help/advising/", "k": "advisor advising drop withdraw hold holds registration register schedule classes degree requirements change major mywsu audit graduation ucore writing portfolio", "s": "Every VCEA student has an assigned advisor (assigned at New Coug Orientation) who helps you plan your degree and stay on track.", "x": "Every VCEA student has an assigned advisor (assigned at New Coug Orientation) who helps you plan your degree and stay on track. Find your advising office Your major Choose your major Architecture Bioengineering Chemical Engineering Civil Engineering Computer Engineering Computer Science Construction Management Cybersecurity Electrical Engineering Interior Design Landscape Architecture Materials Science and Engineering Mechanical Engineering Software Engineering Advisor names and booking links by major: [TBD: add once a single booking page exists]. No JavaScript? Use the table below. Who is my advisor? Advisors are assigned at New Coug Orientation . In myWSU: open the Academic Advising tile, then Academic Progress, then My Academics. [VERIFY: current myWSU steps] Before your appointment Review your Academic Requirements report Bring your questions Check for holds Reading your Academic Requirements report Sign in to myWSU with your WSU Network ID Open the Academic Advising tile Choose Academic Progress in the left menu Open the My Academics tab to see your report Requirements by major Major Advising Schedule of study Architecture Advising Requirements Bioengineering Advising Requirements Chemical Engineering Advising Requirements Civil Engineering Advising Requirements Computer Engineering Advising Requirements Computer Science Advising Requirements Construction Management Advising Requirements Cybersecurity Advising Requirements Electrical Engineering Advising Requirements Int"}, {"t": "Tutoring", "u": "academic-help/tutoring/", "k": "tutor tutoring homework stuck class course study math physics calculus failing behind exam", "s": "Free drop-in, one-on-one peer tutoring for VCEA courses. No appointment needed.", "x": "Free drop-in, one-on-one peer tutoring for VCEA courses. No appointment needed. Bowers Tutoring Center, Schweitzer Engineering Hall (SEH) 130, Monday to Friday, times vary by course (see tutor schedule), Closed when WSU is not holding classes. The tutor schedule is on tutoring.wsu.edu and needs your WSU login. [TBD: publish the schedule here by course so students can check without signing in] How it works Walk in, no appointment. Tutors are trained Voiland College students who have already passed the course they tutor. Stop by early in the semester to see the space and the tutor schedules posted on the wall. What to bring Textbook and notes The assignment you are working on A scientific calculator Specific questions, so the tutor knows where you are stuck Holiday schedule Tutoring runs only on days WSU holds classes, so it is closed on holidays such as Presidents' Day. Courses we cover [TBD: list or link] Meet your tutors Aiden Vezzetti Computer Science Courses: [TBD] Annalee Nersten Computer Science Courses: [TBD] Atticus Prim Electrical Engineering Courses: [TBD] Audrey Scott Civil Engineering Courses: [TBD] Caleb Goin Mechanical Engineering Courses: [TBD] Chloe Wallace Civil Engineering Courses: [TBD] Curtis Cummings Mechanical Engineering Courses: [TBD] Kobe Bui Mechanical Engineering Courses: [TBD] Matt Jordan Architecture (M.Arch) Courses: [TBD] Maura Castillo Chemical Engineering Courses: [TBD] Samuel Faville Mechanical Engineering Courses: [TBD] Savannah Frey Mechanic"}, {"t": "Mentoring", "u": "academic-help/mentoring/", "k": "mentor mentoring peer boeing swe network", "s": "Get connected with a peer or a working engineer who has been where you are.", "x": "Get connected with a peer or a working engineer who has been where you are. Voiland Peer Network Who it is for First-year and transfer students What you get An experienced undergraduate peer for guidance, resources, and practice with communication and leadership How to apply Use the Get started button Deadline [TBD] Contact [TBD: contact] Get started SWE Mentoring Program Who it is for First-year engineering students What you get A practicing woman engineer who stays in touch through college: email check-ins, meetups during breaks, job shadowing, facility tours, campus luncheons How to apply Email the program Deadline [TBD] Contact wsu.swe.mentor@wsu.edu Boeing Mentorship Program Who it is for Engineering students What you get A Boeing mentor in your field, a virtual kickoff and networking, Boeing speaker events, and possibly a midyear group event How to apply Online application (hide button when closed) Deadline [TBD: 2026-27 dates; live page still shows Aug 29, 2025] Contact vcea.internships@wsu.edu , 509-335-8726 [VERIFY: live page links tel to 509-335-3740]"}, {"t": "First-Year & Transfer", "u": "first-year-transfer/", "k": "", "s": "Welcome to Voiland College. Here is how to get started, whether you came straight from high school or transferred in.", "x": "Welcome to Voiland College. Here is how to get started, whether you came straight from high school or transferred in. First-Year Program ENGR 101 and 102: your first courses in the college. Learn more Transfer Students Credits, advising and getting connected fast. Learn more New Student Checklist What to do before classes and in your first month. Open checklist Your first semester at a glance Your advisor is assigned at New Coug Orientation. Find your advising office . Free drop-in tutoring is in SEH 130, Monday to Friday. Tutoring . A peer mentor who has been where you are. Mentoring . 40+ clubs, open to every student with no experience needed. Clubs . Questions? First-year students: Kimberlee Hughes, kimberlee.hughes@wsu.edu . Transfer students: Alena Hume, alena.hume@wsu.edu ."}, {"t": "First-Year Program (ENGR 101/102)", "u": "first-year-transfer/first-year-program/", "k": "first-year freshman engr 101 102", "s": "ENGR 101 and ENGR 102 are Voiland College's first-year courses, part of the First Year Inquiry (FYI) program. They introduce the college's support services, the skills engineering ", "x": "ENGR 101 and ENGR 102 are Voiland College's first-year courses, part of the First Year Inquiry (FYI) program. They introduce the college's support services, the skills engineering students need, and the range of majors you can choose from. The courses Course Theme What you do ENGR 101 Starting your engineering portfolio Make the move from high school to engineering: WSU resources, study habits and your first résumé ENGR 102 Filling your engineering portfolio Explore the disciplines and majors and build your portfolio with projects ENGR 107 Math for engineering For students who are not yet calculus-ready: math concepts tied to engineering problems ENGR 390 Engineering Leadership Leadership and professional skills; also trains the peer coordinators who lead ENGR 101 and 102 sections What a week looks like A discussion session led by a peer coordinator “Experience Engineering” trips and tours of labs and facilities Team projects built in the Frank Innovation Zone (FIZ) [VERIFY: current days and format with the course instructor] Who teaches it Kimberlee Hughes is the instructor. Every section is led by a peer coordinator, an upper-division engineering student who is your first point of contact. How it fits your major Check your major's schedule of study to see where ENGR 101 and 102 fit. In Bioengineering, they replace ENGR 120. [VERIFY: which majors require the sequence] Find your major's requirements . Become a peer coordinator Upper-division students can lead a first-year sec"}, {"t": "Transfer Students", "u": "first-year-transfer/transfer-students/", "k": "transfer credit credits equivalency community college", "s": "Transferring into Voiland College? Start with the transfer coordinator, check how your credits came in, and get connected in your first weeks.", "x": "Transferring into Voiland College? Start with the transfer coordinator, check how your credits came in, and get connected in your first weeks. Your first steps Meet the transfer coordinator, Alena Hume, to review your plan. Open your Academic Requirements report in myWSU and check how each transfer course was counted. If a course did not transfer the way you expected, bring its syllabus to the transfer coordinator. [VERIFY: review process] Meet your major's advising office to plan the semesters ahead. Join the Voiland Peer Network , which matches transfer students with an experienced peer. Credits and equivalencies WSU's transfer course equivalency tool shows how courses from other schools count at WSU. [TBD: link]. Certification into your major depends on specific courses and grades, so check your major's requirements early. Scholarships for transfers Pullman transfer students who have completed the calculus series and the calculus-based physics or chemistry series with a 3.4 GPA or higher are considered for the Dean's Scholarship. Scholarships . Get connected fast Pick a club in your field: club directory Build something in the FIZ Drop in at Career Services in SEH 102 about internships Questions transfers ask Do I take ENGR 101 and 102? It depends on your major and what transferred. Ask the transfer coordinator. [VERIFY] My course came in as elective credit. Can that change? Sometimes. Bring the syllabus to the transfer coordinator for a review."}, {"t": "New Student Checklist", "u": "first-year-transfer/new-student-checklist/", "k": "", "s": "Ten things to do in your first semester, in order.", "x": "Ten things to do in your first semester, in order. Before classes start Find your advising office and note your advisor's name Open your Academic Requirements report in myWSU Save the hours and locations page on your phone First two weeks Visit tutoring in SEH 130 before you need it, so you know the space Find a club and go to one meeting (club fair date: [TBD]) Stop by the Wellness Center in Daggy Hall 101 First month Create your Handshake profile and upload a résumé Visit the FIZ in Dana Hall Before you register for next semester Check myWSU for registration holds Meet your advisor and bring your questions Apply for scholarships by the January 31 priority deadline: Scholarships"}, {"t": "Careers & Internships", "u": "careers/", "k": "job jobs internship internships career coach handshake co-op employer", "s": "Career coaching, internships, career fairs, and job search tools for Voiland College students.", "x": "Career coaching, internships, career fairs, and job search tools for Voiland College students. SEH Welcome Center, Room 102, 150 SE Spokane St. [VERIFY: Mentoring page still says Dana 140], Peer Career Guide drop-ins: Mon 2–3, Tue 12–1, Wed 3–4, Thu 12–1, Fri 2–3 Right now: Mon 2 to 3, Tue 12 to 1, Wed 3 to 4, Thu 12 to 1, Fri 2 to 3 Who we serve: In-person support for Pullman and Bremerton students; remote support for students at other WSU campuses. What we do Career coaching and mock interviews Technical career fairs and employer information sessions Networking events and industry tours Job postings and on-campus interviews The ProPEL internship credit program Career Coaching One-on-one coaching and mock interviews. Meet coaches Internships & ProPEL Earn credit for a paid internship. Learn more Career Fairs Dates, prep, and FAQs. See fairs Career Toolkit Résumés, interviews, job search. Open toolkit Careers Blog Tips and news. Read"}, {"t": "Career Coaching", "u": "careers/coaching/", "k": "coach coaching appointment book drop-in mock interview", "s": "What a coaching session covers, and how to book one.", "x": "What a coaching session covers, and how to book one. Drop-ins at SEH 102 (Peer Career Guides): Mon 2–3, Tue 12–1, Wed 3–4, Thu 12–1, Fri 2–3 What a session covers Clarifying your career goals Résumé, cover letter and LinkedIn review Finding internships and jobs Interview practice Planning your next step Book a time with the Director or a Peer Career Guide, or drop in at SEH 102 during guide hours. Meet the coaches (single list, maintained here only) Lisa Hathaway Director Book Chance Shungu Peer Career Guide, CS Book Samantha Klein Peer Career Guide, CS Book Cheri Heng Peer Career Guide, ME Book Gavin O’Connor Peer Career Guide, EE Book Mock interviews Practice with a coach before the real thing. Book a session above and mention the role you are interviewing for."}, {"t": "Internships & ProPEL", "u": "careers/internships-propel/", "k": "propel credit engr 488 489 internship co-op paid", "s": "ProPEL lets you earn credit (ENGR 488/489) for a paid internship or co-op.", "x": "ProPEL lets you earn credit (ENGR 488/489) for a paid internship or co-op. How it works Get an offer. Pay is required, and you need a worksite mentor. Apply online as soon as you have the offer, and ask your employer to complete the employer form . Both forms are needed. Enroll in ENGR 488 (or your major's cross-listed 488) while you work, then ENGR 489 the semester after. Good to know Placements run 12 weeks to 12 months, and many employers prefer six months or longer. You can stay a full-time WSU student while on a full-time internship. Talk to your advisor first about time to degree. How credit counts toward your major varies by department. Eligibility At least 420 hours (full time up to 12 months, or part time across terms) Paid (wage, stipend, or fellowship) A named mentor/supervisor at the worksite At least 50% related to your major On-site (virtual case by case) Enroll in ENGR 488 (2 cr) during the work term and ENGR 489 (1 cr) afterward Credit and costs Tuition applies like any course. Full-time work earns 2 credits of 488, part-time 1. Both 488 and 489 are needed for the tech/free elective. [VERIFY: live page contradicts itself on “one or two credit” and 30-hour thresholds] Financial aid note Co-op income earned while enrolled in ProPEL has not counted against your aid calculation. [VERIFY: confirm wording against the current Student Aid Index rules with Financial Aid] FAQ Do I need to take other classes? No. Other WSU courses are not required while you are in ProPEL"}, {"t": "Career Fairs", "u": "careers/career-fairs/", "k": "career fair fairs expo employers recruiters", "s": "Meet employers, practice your pitch, and find your next internship.", "x": "Meet employers, practice your pitch, and find your next internship. Upcoming fairs No fairs listed yet. [TBD: spring 2027 dates, one Handshake link per fair. Fall 2026 fairs, the Sept 9 Internship Pop-Up and the Oct 6 Technical Fair, are past.] What to expect WSU Career Expos run every fall and spring through the Academic Success and Career Center, and Voiland College hosts a Technical Fair. The Virtual Expo runs on Handshake with group sessions and one-on-one chats with recruiters. It is an introduction, not an interview. Freshmen are welcome and encouraged to come. Prepare in 5 steps Update your résumé and get it reviewed in SEH 102 Complete your Handshake profile Pick the employers you want to meet and read up on them Practice a 30-second introduction Plan your outfit At the fair Arrive early and start with shorter lines. Shake hands, introduce yourself and hand over your résumé. Ask about the application process and collect a contact name from everyone you meet. Be considerate of the students waiting behind you. Skip salary and benefits questions. Good questions to ask What do you look for in candidates? What training is there for entry-level hires? What assignments would I get? What skills matter most for success here? After the fair Send a thank-you note with your résumé attached, keep researching the company, and book a mock interview. FAQ How often are fairs held? Every fall and spring. [VERIFY: Voiland pages say both bi-annual and tri-annual] What should I wear? Prof"}, {"t": "Career Toolkit", "u": "careers/toolkit/", "k": "", "s": "Tools to get you from résumé to offer.", "x": "Tools to get you from résumé to offer. Start with your résumé, practice for interviews, then use the search tools. Any time you want feedback, book a coach or drop in at SEH 102. Résumés & Cover Letters Bullet formula, action verbs, tips. Open Interviews & Offers Prepare, perform, negotiate. Open Job & Internship Search Tools grouped by purpose. Open"}, {"t": "Résumés & Cover Letters", "u": "careers/toolkit/resumes-cover-letters/", "k": "resume resumes cv cover letter", "s": "Build a résumé that fits the job, then get it reviewed.", "x": "Build a résumé that fits the job, then get it reviewed. List education and experience that fits the job Start each bullet with an action verb and add a result Proofread, then ask someone else to proofread Drop in for a review, then upload to Handshake and set your profile to visible Getting started Write down a professional inventory of your projects, jobs, clubs and skills first. Build your own document instead of a template, because templates are hard to change when you add experience. The bullet formula Action verb, plus what you did, plus the result. Basic Stronger Assisted with inventory Assisted with weekly and monthly inventory of 500+ medical supplies Worked to revise website Reduced site load time by 5 seconds, increasing page traffic by 25 percent Action verbs Analyzed, Assembled, Built, Calibrated, Coordinated, Designed, Developed, Directed, Evaluated, Fabricated, Improved, Installed, Led, Managed, Modeled, Organized, Programmed, Reduced, Researched, Supervised, Tested, Trained, Wrote. Cover letters Write it after your résumé and tailor it to each posting. Show how your experience solves the employer's problem. Use numbers, dollars or percentages. Use your own words and let your personality show. Use a clear font such as Times New Roman, Arial or Helvetica. Book a coach"}, {"t": "Interviews & Offers", "u": "careers/toolkit/interviews-offers/", "k": "interview interviews offer salary negotiate star", "s": "From the first screen to the signed offer.", "x": "From the first screen to the signed offer. Know the format Practice common questions Research the company Send a thank-you note Types of interviews Screening call, 30 to 45 minutes One-way recorded video Live video or phone: quiet place, charged device, résumé and notes in reach On-site: keep receipts if the company covers travel Panel or peer group: speak to everyone Over a meal: manners count Second interview: longer, more people Before Read the job description and prepare an example for each requirement. Learn the organization's mission and recent news. Do a dry run of the route or the video link. During Arrive about 15 minutes early. Be honest, emphasize the positive, and when asked about a weakness, name something you are improving. Ask about next steps and do not raise salary first. What to wear Dress professionally. The Career Cougar Closet at the Academic Success and Career Center lends professional clothing. Thank-you notes [TBD: short guidance] Negotiating salary and benefits [TBD: salary research sites] Accepting or declining an offer [TBD] Schedule a mock interview Book a coach"}, {"t": "Job & Internship Search", "u": "careers/toolkit/job-search/", "k": "job search handshake linkedin careershift forage parker dewey", "s": "Tools in three groups.", "x": "Tools in three groups. Search Handshake : the main place for WSU internships, co-ops and jobs CareerShift : company research and contacts, plus email campaigns you can track Job Search Tracker: a daily-updated spreadsheet by major, with a WSU login Parker Dewey : paid micro-internships The Forage : free job simulations Company career pages LinkedIn Jobs and Internships Internships abroad Regional boards: Washington WorkSource, Idaho Works, state and federal government jobs, Aerospace Career Hub Explore careers Vault: employer rankings, company reviews and industry guides O*NET Interest Profiler (My Next Move): matches your interests to occupations What Can I Do With This Major?: careers by major (external site, majors may differ from WSU) CareerOneStop: U.S. Department of Labor tools for careers, training and jobs Track and network Job Search Tracker Email finders such as hunter.io and Anymail Finder for reaching alumni Professional Applicant Profile Portal for employers to find you Book a coach"}, {"t": "Careers Blog", "u": "careers/blog/", "k": "", "s": "Short, practical career advice from Career Services.", "x": "Short, practical career advice from Career Services. Recent posts 5 Things Every New Grad Should Do This Summer Create Demand for Your Expertise How to Write the Perfect Follow-up Email After an Interview Looking for an Internship? Here's How to Ask for Help That Actually Works [TBD: link each post; replace with the live category feed] Want help with what you read? Book a career coach or drop in at SEH 102."}, {"t": "Paying for School", "u": "paying-for-school/", "k": "", "s": "Scholarships and emergency aid.", "x": "Scholarships and emergency aid. Most Voiland College scholarships come from one application, due January 31. For a sudden expense, ask for emergency aid. Scholarships One application for most Voiland College scholarships. Learn more Emergency Aid Help when something unexpected happens. Learn more WSU Financial Aid Comparing schools? See what Voiland College really costs ."}, {"t": "Scholarships", "u": "paying-for-school/scholarships/", "k": "money scholarship scholarships fafsa aid tuition deans wuee pay cost", "s": "One application, the WSU General Scholarship Application, puts you in the running for the Distinguished Dean's, Dean's and WUEE scholarships.", "x": "One application, the WSU General Scholarship Application, puts you in the running for the Distinguished Dean's, Dean's and WUEE scholarships. Priority deadline: January 31 for the following academic year. [TBD: confirm year] Distinguished Dean's Scholarship The college's top award: full tuition, renewable for up to four years. Finalists come from Dean's Scholarship applicants and are chosen for academic excellence, curiosity, leadership, and a clear sense of purpose. Dean's Scholarship More than 300 awarded each year to incoming first-year and transfer students. First-years are considered on high school GPA and test scores. Pullman transfer students need the calculus series and either the calculus-based physics or chemistry series, with a 3.4 GPA or higher. Apply early, since funds are limited. WUEE Scholarship Out-of-state engineering and computer science students at WSU Pullman can add $2,000 a year to their Dean's Scholarship for each year they qualify. School and department scholarships Open to students certified in a major. Dates and methods vary, so check with your school. Civil and Environmental Engineering Voiland School of Chemical Engineering and Bioengineering School of Design and Construction Electrical Engineering and Computer Science Mechanical and Materials Engineering More opportunities Air Force ROTC Scholarships LSAMP Research Scholarship Washington State Opportunity Scholarship WSU Scholarships"}, {"t": "Emergency Aid", "u": "paying-for-school/emergency-aid/", "k": "emergency money rent food bills broke aid", "s": "Help for an unexpected cost that puts your classes at risk. [TBD: final wording once the VCEA emergency scholarship policy is approved]", "x": "Help for an unexpected cost that puts your classes at risk. [TBD: final wording once the VCEA emergency scholarship policy is approved] Who can ask Voiland College students facing a sudden, one-time expense. [TBD: eligibility from the policy] What it can help with [TBD: covered costs from the policy]. If you are not sure whether your situation fits, ask anyway. How to ask Email vcea.info@wsu.edu with “Emergency aid” in the subject. Say briefly what happened, what it costs, and when you need the money. Someone from Student Success will contact you. [TBD: response time] Privacy Requests are handled by a small number of staff and are not shared with your instructors. [VERIFY: confidentiality language with the policy] Other help right now WSU Office of Student Financial Aid: ask about adjusting your aid package [TBD: link] WSU Dean of Students emergency and basic needs resources [TBD: link] Wellness Center if the stress is building Scholarships for longer-term costs Questions Is it a loan? [TBD: per policy] Can it affect my financial aid? Outside aid can change an aid package. Check with Financial Aid. [VERIFY]"}, {"t": "Get Involved", "u": "get-involved/", "k": "", "s": "Clubs, making, entrepreneurship, and events.", "x": "Clubs, making, entrepreneurship, and events. Clubs, maker spaces and events are where most students find their people and their first real projects. Every club is open to any enrolled WSU student. Student Clubs 40+ clubs. Browse Maker Spaces & FIZ Build something. Explore Entrepreneurship Frank Institute. Learn more Events What's coming up. See events Student Spotlights Meet your peers. Read"}, {"t": "Student Clubs", "u": "get-involved/clubs/", "k": "club clubs rso join society team organization", "s": "More than 40 Voiland College clubs, from design teams to professional societies. All are open to any enrolled WSU student, with no experience needed.", "x": "More than 40 Voiland College clubs, from design teams to professional societies. All are open to any enrolled WSU student, with no experience needed. Three ways to join Email the club using the directory below. Search WSU Presence for club profiles and events. [VERIFY: link] Ask the Club Hub in SEH 202 or at vcea.studentclubs@wsu.edu . Club directory Filter clubs Club Contact Faculty advisor 3D Printing Club 3DPrinting.vcea@wsu.edu Arda Gozen Amateur Radio Club (Rho Epsilon or Ham Radio) hamradio.vcea@wsu.edu Mae Siev American Institute of Architecture Students (AIAS) aias.vcea@wsu.edu Hala Barakat American Institute of Chemical Engineers (AIChE) aiche.vcea@wsu.edu Colin Lehman-Chong American Society of Civil Engineers (ASCE) asce.vcea@wsu.edu Karl Olsen American Society of Landscape Architecture at WSU (ASLA) asla.vcea@wsu.edu Steve Austin American Society of Mechanical Engineers (ASME) asme.vcea@wsu.edu Roland Chen American Society of Mechanical Engineers (ASME) – Bremerton Campus [TBD] TBD Associated Students of Construction Management (ASCM) ascm.vcea@wsu.edu Darhl Edwards Association for Computing Machinery (ACM) acm.vcea@wsu.edu Venera Arnaoudova Biomedical Engineering Society (BMES) bmes.vcea@wsu.edu Dmitri Tolkatchev Concrete Canoe ConcreteCanoe.vcea@wsu.edu Karl Olsen Cougar Car Club (CCC) vcea.CougarCarClub@wsu.edu Mae Siev Cougs in Space – CougSat CougsInSpace.vcea@wsu.edu Venera Arnaoudova Crimson Robotics [TBD] Mae Siev Cyber Security Group (CSG) csg.vcea@wsu.edu"}, {"t": "Maker Spaces & FIZ", "u": "get-involved/maker-spaces/", "k": "3d print printer laser shop fiz machine build fabrication maker", "s": "Three places to build, with trained staff and real equipment.", "x": "Three places to build, with trained staff and real equipment. Frank Innovation Zone (FIZ) What it is A maker space with fabrication tools to bring ideas to life. Who can use it WSU students, faculty and staff Access / training [TBD] Hours [TBD] Location Dana Hall, ground floor FIZ website Fabrication Labs What it is About 3,500 square feet of maker space with digital fabrication run by trained shop technicians, plus a Model Shop with scale analog machines. Who can use it [TBD] Access / training [TBD] Hours [TBD] Location Next to Carpenter Hall Fabrication Labs Voiland College Shops What it is Shops that fabricate parts, tools and machines for research and competitions. Who can use it Faculty, graduate students and student clubs Access / training [TBD] Hours [TBD] Location Dana Hall, ground floor"}, {"t": "Entrepreneurship", "u": "get-involved/entrepreneurship/", "k": "", "s": "The Harold Frank Engineering Entrepreneurship Institute brings engineering, business and communications students together to turn technology ideas into ventures.", "x": "The Harold Frank Engineering Entrepreneurship Institute brings engineering, business and communications students together to turn technology ideas into ventures. What students can do Join a cross-disciplinary team with business and communications students Compete in the business plan competition (past teams, such as PicsGenie, have won awards) Get mentoring on taking a product to market [TBD: confirm current offerings] Find funding for early prototypes [TBD] Build your prototype Use the FIZ and fabrication labs to make the first version of your idea. Related Student clubs for teammates and projects Career Services for startup internships"}, {"t": "Events", "u": "get-involved/events/", "k": "", "s": "Signature Voiland College events through the year, plus the live events feed.", "x": "Signature Voiland College events through the year, plus the live events feed. Innovation Expo Each spring, clubs show their projects in Beasley Coliseum. Judges give a Dean's Award to the club that best represents the college, presented at the Student Excellence Awards. 2027 date: [TBD] Details Senior Capstone Expo Seniors present their capstone design projects. April 15, [TBD: year and location] Details Career Fairs WSU Career Expos each fall and spring, plus the Technical Fair. See fairs Career Networking Mixer Meet employers in a relaxed setting. [TBD: date] Careers Evening with Industry Club-sponsored evenings where students talk with professionals in their field. [TBD: dates by club] Clubs Ice Cream Socials Meet the Student Success team and other students. [TBD: dates] Details All upcoming events [TBD: automatic feed of student events; hide this section when empty] Hosting an event? Clubs can promote events on the college's digital signage. See flyer specs ."}, {"t": "Student Spotlights", "u": "get-involved/spotlights/", "k": "", "s": "Voiland College students on what has helped them succeed.", "x": "Voiland College students on what has helped them succeed. Betty Mitchell Civil engineering Graduating December 2026. [TBD: link to story] Brandon Swinger Civil engineering Graduating fall 2028. [TBD: link to story] Alex Brown Materials science Graduating fall 2026. [TBD: link to story] Connor Reschke Civil engineering Graduating fall 2026. [TBD: link to story] Lauren Hoyt Wings Club Scholarship recipient [TBD: link to story] Trinity Middleton Materials science and engineering Graduating May 2029. [TBD: link to story] Ashley McCoy Architectural studies Graduating May 2028. [TBD: link to story] David Montes-Vilanova Computer science Graduating May 2028. [TBD: link to story] Brian Romero Gomez Mechanical engineering Graduating May 2027. [TBD: link to story] Nominate a student Know a student whose story would help others? Send their name, major and a sentence about why to vcea.info@wsu.edu . [TBD: nomination form]"}, {"t": "Wellness", "u": "wellness/", "k": "stress anxiety counseling mental health overwhelmed relax talk crisis 988 sleep lonely", "s": "A free space in Voiland College to relax, recharge, or talk with a trained peer supporter. It is not therapy or long-term counseling.", "x": "A free space in Voiland College to relax, recharge, or talk with a trained peer supporter. It is not therapy or long-term counseling. Daggy Hall 101, Monday to Friday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m. Closed weekends, university holidays and closures. After hours, call (509) 335-2159 for Counseling and Psychological Services. Right now: Mon to Fri, 9 to 4 In crisis? Call or text 988 , call 911 , or go to your nearest emergency room. After-hours counseling: (509) 335-2159 Wellness rooms Relaxation Room A quiet room for mindfulness, with a massage chair, aromatherapy, relaxation sounds and soft lighting. Art Room Drop in and make something. Art supplies are on hand to help you decompress. Game Room The largest room, with LED lighting, a TV, floor gaming chairs, tables and a Wii for active games. How to use the center Ask a Peer Supporter at the front desk which room is open. Check in with the QR code at the desk. Head to your room and settle in. Peer support conversations Talk with a trained Peer Supporter or practicum student from psychology, human development and related fields. Good topics: exam or homework stress, time management, lack of sleep, life changes and basic needs. The center is a place to connect and de-stress. It does not replace counseling. Become a peer supporter or practicum student Psychology, social work and similar majors, or anyone who wants to support fellow Cougs, are welcome to apply. [TBD: how to apply]"}, {"t": "About & Contact", "u": "about/", "k": "", "s": "Who we are and how to reach us.", "x": "Who we are and how to reach us. The Student Success team runs advising support, tutoring, career services, clubs, the FIZ and the Wellness Center for Voiland College students. Our Team Directory by area. Meet the team Locations & Hours One table for every office. See locations Fastest ways to reach us vcea.info@wsu.edu 509-335-5050 What to do if you are stuck Find us in SEH Schweitzer Engineering Hall (SEH) 150 SE Spokane St., Pullman Most Student Success services are in one building. 2nd floor 202 Club Hub 1st floor 102 Welcome Center and Career Services 124 and 126 Advising and Student Success offices 130 Bowers Tutoring Center Get directions In other buildings: Wellness Center : Daggy Hall 101 FIZ and Voiland College Shops : Dana Hall, ground floor Fabrication Labs : Next to Carpenter Hall [TBD: photo of the SEH entrance; optional campus map embed in WordPress]"}, {"t": "Our Team", "u": "about/team/", "k": "staff directory contact email phone people", "s": "[TBD: one-line mission]", "x": "[TBD: one-line mission] Leadership Partha Pratim Pande Dean pande@wsu.edu 509-335-5593 SEH 230P Krishnamoorthy Sivakumar Associate Dean, Academic Affairs siva@wsu.edu 509-335-4969 SEH 230R Steve Saunders Associate Dean for Student Success [TBD: email, phone, office] Recruiting Lisa Carmack Student Success Coordinator, Future Cougs lisa.carmack@wsu.edu 509-335-4823 SEH 102A First-Year Programs Kimberlee Hughes Instructor, First-Year Engineering kimberlee.hughes@wsu.edu 509-335-2888 SEH 126E Advising Kasey Schertenleib Director, Advising and Enrollment Management kasey@wsu.edu 509-335-6613 SEH 126C Alena Hume Academic Advisor and Transfer Coordinator alena.hume@wsu.edu 509-335-5376 SEH 126A Brenda Schuster Academic Success Advisor brenda.schuster@wsu.edu 509-335-3060 SEH 124A Tutoring & Retention [TBD: tutoring coordinator] Career Services Lisa Hathaway Director of Internships and Career Services vcea.internships@wsu.edu 509-335-8726 SEH 126D [VERIFY: directory shows a different email address] Clubs and Events Gary Offerdahl Student Success Coordinator, Clubs and Events gary.offerdahl@wsu.edu 509-335-6643 SEH 202A Frank Innovation Zone Maynard Siev FIZ Manager maynard.siev@wsu.edu 509-335-7229 Dana Hall 15 Wellness Alena Hume Director, VCEA Wellness Center alena.hume@wsu.edu (509) 335-5376 Daggy Hall 130 Ria Dominic Graduate Assistant ria.dominic@wsu.edu Daggy Hall 133 Photos [TBD]. Alena Hume is listed as Advisor/Transfer Coordinator in the staff directory and as Wellness Cent"}, {"t": "Locations & Hours", "u": "about/locations/", "k": "hours location where open office address map", "s": "The single source of truth for every office.", "x": "The single source of truth for every office. Find us in SEH Schweitzer Engineering Hall (SEH) 150 SE Spokane St., Pullman Most Student Success services are in one building. 2nd floor 202 Club Hub 1st floor 102 Welcome Center and Career Services 124 and 126 Advising and Student Success offices 130 Bowers Tutoring Center Get directions In other buildings: Wellness Center : Daggy Hall 101 FIZ and Voiland College Shops : Dana Hall, ground floor Fabrication Labs : Next to Carpenter Hall [TBD: photo of the SEH entrance; optional campus map embed in WordPress] Service Location Hours Right now Email Phone Main office [TBD] [TBD] vcea.info@wsu.edu 509-335-5050 Tutoring SEH 130 Mon to Fri, times vary by course vcea.info@wsu.edu 509-335-5050 Career drop-in SEH 102 Mon 2 to 3, Tue 12 to 1, Wed 3 to 4, Thu 12 to 1, Fri 2 to 3 Mon 2 to 3, Tue 12 to 1, Wed 3 to 4, Thu 12 to 1, Fri 2 to 3 vcea.internships@wsu.edu 509-335-8726 Campus visits [TBD] Noon Mon to Sat, 2:30 pm Fri Noon Mon to Sat, 2:30 pm Fri VCEA.Recruiting@wsu.edu [TBD] Club Hub SEH 202 [TBD] vcea.studentclubs@wsu.edu 509-335-6643 FIZ and maker spaces Dana Hall [TBD] mpepper@wsu.edu 509-335-8550 Wellness Center Daggy Hall 101 Mon to Fri, 9 to 4 Mon to Fri, 9 to 4 alena.hume@wsu.edu 509-335-5376 “Right now” uses Pacific time. Map [TBD: map embed] Mailing addresses PO Box 642250, Pullman, WA 99164-2250. Career Services: PO Box 642718, Pullman, WA 99164-2718. Club packages: see Club Advisors ."}, {"t": "Employers", "u": "employers/", "k": "", "s": "Recruit Voiland College students.", "x": "Recruit Voiland College students. Interview on campus or online Career Services provides interview space on the Pullman campus. Schedule on-campus and virtual interviews through Handshake. Host an event Request an information session, pre-interview night, table in a high-traffic area, coffee chat, virtual tour or networking night through Handshake. For on-campus events the Employer Relations Team follows up on space and parking. Recruit at college events Career Expo each fall and spring, the Career Networking Mixer, and club-sponsored Evening with Industry events. Host a ProPEL intern Students earn credit for paid internships. Complete the employer form when a student asks. Volunteer Workshop presentations Career and alumni panels Mock interviews and portfolio reviews Job shadowing Career Trek and industry tours for student groups Professional Applicant Profile Portal Browse approved student and alumni profiles. [TBD: link] Recruiting guidelines Open and fair access for all undergraduate and graduate students. Recruiters follow the WSU Non-Discrimination Policy and NACE principles. [TBD: link] Sponsor a club or Expo [TBD]"}, {"t": "Faculty & Staff", "u": "faculty-staff/", "k": "", "s": "Ways to connect your students with support.", "x": "Ways to connect your students with support. Refer a student (EARS) Use the Early Academic Referral System when a student is missing class, falling behind, or seems to be struggling outside class. A referral reaches the student's advisor and support staff, who reach out to the student. [TBD: link and what the student hears] Teach a first-year section ENGR 101 and 102 sections are led by peer coordinators. Recommend strong upper-division students for the role. First-Year Program . Invite Career Services to your class or club Missing a class? Career Services can cover internships and how to find one, résumés and cover letters, interviewing, professional etiquette and networking. A visit can last from 5 minutes to a full hour (up to 50 minutes for clubs). Request a presentation Form fields: name, email, phone, class or group, topic, location, participants, date, time, length. [TBD: embed or link] Refer students to tutoring Send them to Tutoring in SEH 130. Capstone and Expo involvement [TBD]"}, {"t": "Club Advisors & Officers", "u": "club-advisors/", "k": "", "s": "Everything a club needs to register, fund itself, promote events, and get mail.", "x": "Everything a club needs to register, fund itself, promote events, and get mail. Register or renew as an RSO Voiland College clubs register or renew WSU Registered Student Organization status at the start of each fall semester. New clubs should register as soon as possible, because RSO training dates are limited. Not sure of your status? Email Student Engagement Services at getinvolved@wsu.edu . Funding Student Engagement Services runs the club funding opportunities. Purchase, food and travel requests go through the college's club purchasing system. [TBD: link and current steps] Promote your club Digital signage on Voiland College monitors Design the flyer at 16:9 (PowerPoint) proportions. Add a QR code to a page with dates and details, so one flyer works all term. Tell us the date to take it down. Say whether you want it printed for physical flyer boards (up to 20 prints per club). Email the flyer to vcea.studentclubs@wsu.edu . WSU marketing resources RSOs can use WSU design and printing services. [TBD: link] Reserve a room Book a WSU classroom for meetings and events. [TBD: link] Safety manual and liability forms [TBD: when finalized] Mailing Packages: “Club Name” c/o VCEA Club Hub, Washington State University, 100 Dairy Road, 202 SEH, Pullman, WA 99164-2718 Correspondence: “Club Name” c/o VCEA Club Hub, Washington State University, PO Box 642718, Pullman, WA 99164-2718"}, {"t": "Give", "u": "give/", "k": "", "s": "Your gift reaches students directly: club competitions, the Innovation Expo, emergency aid and the Wellness Center.", "x": "Your gift reaches students directly: club competitions, the Innovation Expo, emergency aid and the Wellness Center. What gifts support Student clubs Travel to national competitions, project materials and tools for 40+ clubs. Give to a club Emergency aid One-time help so a sudden expense does not end a student's semester. Give Wellness Center A space to relax, recharge and talk with a peer supporter. Give Innovation Expo The spring showcase where clubs present their work. Sponsor Other ways to help Donate materials, equipment or services: contact Fernanda Amaral, f.amaral@wsu.edu Mentor a student: Mentoring Host interns or recruit: Employers Impact [TBD: one donor or student story, and a few numbers such as clubs supported or students helped]"}, {"t": "Visit Voiland College", "u": "visit/", "k": "tour tours visit prospective campus future cougs high school", "s": "Prospective students: come see the college. Sign up for a visit by email.", "x": "Prospective students: come see the college. Sign up for a visit by email. Noon visit: Monday through Saturday. 2:30 pm visit: Fridays only. Pacific time. Meeting spot: [TBD: building and room]. Next visit: Noon Mon to Sat, 2:30 pm Fri Pick a time Visit Days 12:00 pm (noon) Monday through Saturday 2:30 pm Fridays only How to sign up Email VCEA.Recruiting@wsu.edu with the day and time you want. Include your name, how many people are coming, and your intended major. [TBD: confirm what the team needs] The team replies to confirm your visit. [TBD: how far ahead to ask] What to expect [TBD: what the visit includes, how long it lasts, parking and where to check in] [VERIFY: the request spelled the address “VCEA.Recuriting@wsu.edu”; this page uses VCEA.Recruiting@wsu.edu. Confirm which is correct.] Wondering about cost? See what families actually pay and what graduates earn: Why Voiland College Costs Less ."}, {"t": "What to do if", "u": "what-to-do/", "k": "help stuck failing drop withdraw hold change major emergency overwhelmed who ask transfer credits lost confused", "s": "Start with your situation. Each one lists the first step and where to go.", "x": "Start with your situation. Each one lists the first step and where to go. I'm behind or failing in a class Go to tutoring this week, then talk with your instructor. If you are thinking about dropping, see your advisor before the deadline. Tutoring Academic calendar I have a registration hold Open myWSU to see which office placed the hold. Your advisor can help you clear advising holds. myWSU Academic Advising I want to drop a class or withdraw Talk with your advisor first. Dropping can affect financial aid, scholarships and your degree timeline. Find your advising office Academic calendar I want to change my major Meet with an advisor in the major you want. Each school sets its own certification requirements. Requirements by major I'm a transfer and my credits didn't count Contact the transfer coordinator with your transcript and the course you expected to transfer. Transfer Students I have a money emergency Tell us what happened. Emergency aid can help with unexpected costs. Emergency Aid Scholarships I'm stressed or overwhelmed Stop by the Wellness Center to take a break or talk with a peer supporter. In a crisis, call or text 988. Wellness Center Call or text 988 I need an internship or job Drop in at SEH 102 or book a career coach. Paid internships can earn credit through ProPEL. Careers & Internships ProPEL I don't know who to ask Email or call the Student Success team and we will point you to the right person. vcea.info@wsu.edu 509-335-5050"}, {"t": "Why Voiland College Costs Less", "u": "cost-and-value/", "k": "uw udub husky huskies seattle university washington compare comparison cost costs tuition price afford affordable net price sticker out-of-state wue salary salaries earnings debt loans value worth roi compare", "s": "Save $22,440 over four years compared with the Seattle flagship, and start at the same salaries in mechanical, civil and electrical engineering.", "x": "Save $22,440 over four years compared with the Seattle flagship, and start at the same salaries in mechanical, civil and electrical engineering. $22,440 less to attend over four years $13,646 less out-of-state tuition, every year 87% of applicants admitted, vs. 39% in Seattle Source: College Scorecard: WSU , College Scorecard: Seattle comparison school . Four-year savings = 4 x the $5,610 yearly difference in average cost of attendance (2024-25). Out-of-state tuition and fees, 2025-26. Admission rates, fall 2024 Lower cost of attendance Per year WSU Pullman Seattle flagship You save at WSU Average cost of attendance (2024-25) $27,914 $33,524 $5,610 Out-of-state tuition and fees (2025-26) $30,994 $44,640 $13,646 Cost of attendance is the full yearly budget: tuition and fees, books and supplies, and living expenses, averaged for full-time first-year students who receive federal aid. Aid keeps pace: the average net price after grants and scholarships for in-state students receiving federal aid is $13,462 at WSU and $13,663 in Seattle (2024-25). Your family's number depends on income, so run WSU's net price calculator. [TBD: link] Source: College Scorecard: WSU , College Scorecard: Seattle comparison school Same starting salaries Major WSU graduates, first year Seattle graduates, first year Mechanical Engineering $78,276 $79,240 Civil Engineering $74,997 $75,992 Electrical Engineering $83,445 $84,874 In mechanical, civil and electrical engineering, WSU graduates' median first-yea"}];